<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title><%= title %></title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/style.css">
</head>
<body>
  <%- include('_menu', { page: 'index' }) %>

  <!-- Hero: the search box is the front door, so it goes first. Typing a room
       here lands straight on it, rather than making people open the map and
       search again once they get there. -->
  <main class="home">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="hero-content">
        <h2>Find Your Way Around BCIT</h2>
        <p>
          Search any room on the Burnaby campus, see it drawn on its floor plan,
          and get walking directions to the door.
        </p>

        <form class="home-search" action="/map" method="get">
          <input type="text" name="room" placeholder="Room or building, e.g. SW3-1600" aria-label="Room or building">
          <button type="submit" class="btn">Search</button>
        </form>

        <div class="hero-links">
          <a href="/map" class="btn secondary">Browse the Map</a>
          <a href="/favorites" class="btn secondary">Your Favourites</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <% if (coverage) { %>
    <section class="home-stats">
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="stat-number"><%= coverage.buildings %></span>
        <span class="stat-label">buildings mapped</span>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="stat-number"><%= coverage.floors %></span>
        <span class="stat-label">floors</span>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="stat-number"><%= coverage.rooms.toLocaleString('en-CA') %></span>
        <span class="stat-label">rooms you can search</span>
      </div>
    </section>
    <% } %>

    <section class="home-features">
      <h3>What You Can Do</h3>
      <div class="feature-grid">
        <article class="feature">
          <h4>Search for a Room</h4>
          <p>
            Type a room number and go straight to it. Every room is drawn in
            place on its own floor plan, so you can see what it is next to
            before you set off.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature">
          <h4>Move Between Floors</h4>
          <p>
            Pick a building and step through its floors. Each floor lists the
            rooms on it, so you can find what you want without hunting across
            the drawing.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature">
          <h4>Get Walking Directions</h4>
          <p>
            Give it two rooms and it routes between them along the corridors,
            taking the stairs or the lift where the floors change, and draws
            the path on the map.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature">
          <h4>Save the Places You Keep Going Back To</h4>
          <p>
            Star a room to keep it on your favourites list. This is the one
            part that needs an account, since it is tied to you.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="home-how">
      <h3>Where the Maps Come From</h3>
      <p>
        The floor plans are built from BCIT's own published architectural
        drawings. Each sheet is read, the rooms are traced from the walls, and
        the result is fitted to the building's real footprint so a room on
        screen sits where it does on campus. Corridors, doorways, stairs and
        lifts are picked out of the same drawings, which is what makes indoor
        routing possible.
      </p>
      <p class="home-note">
        Room labels are read from the drawings automatically, so a few will be
        wrong or missing. If a room looks off, it is the reading of the plan
        that is wrong, not the building.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</body>
</html>
